there are so many spammers on discord they've already spawned the meme `1.jpg, 2.jpg, 3.jpg, 4.jpg @everyone @here`

![](/pictures/it/discord_spam.png)

all because absolutely any unauthorized file read access lets you easily steal a session by grepping for the token once.


# # discord token

a token, very roughly, in discord is just a session hash with NO binding to hardware, network or anything like that.

to get a token you cast `login` + `password` + `2fa` /or `passkey`, then discord hands out a token (a hash based on some data), the token becomes the session - from then on the client sends this token to the server with every request

it's a standard setup: that's how OAuth2, JWT, bearer tokens work - the model itself is fine, the token can be revoked server-side.

: the problem isn't the token, it's where it sits...

# # how discord stores the token

discord stores the token in `Local Storage`, technically a leveldb database sitting in the user's home directory `~/.config/discord/Local Storage/leveldb/`

or on windows: `%APPDATA%/discord/Local Storage/leveldb/`

and the token sits there UNENCRYPTED in plaintext.

: any program running as a user with sufficient permissions (usually discord runs as the same user as all the other programs) - can read this file.

---

the funniest part is that electron has had a built-in `safeStorage` API since 2019,
which can encrypt data through OS-level mechanisms: like `Keychain Access`(macos), `DPAPI`(windows), `kwallet, gnome-libsecret, or freedesktop Secret Service. Depending on the DE`(linux)

i.e. the tool for properly storing secrets is right there in electron out of the box, but discord doesn't do it...

# # how to steal a token

when a token gets compromised - NOTHING HAPPENS.

anyone takes the token, feeds it to another client, and T H A T ' S I T...- you're let into the account.

![](/pictures/it/discord_token_login.webp)

just without a login, password, 2fa, sms, passkey, push-notification, confirmation, new login alerts and so on.

![](/pictures/it/discord_2fa.webp)

the new login isn't even registered as new, the old session just *moves* to another machine (the previous one gets logged out of the app)

i.e. all you can notice is getting logged out of your device, and only if someone has already used that token rather than saving it for a rainy day.

# # how cheap the theft is

you do this:

![](/pictures/it/discord_grep_token.webp)

works exactly the same on windows - any program running as the same user can 99.99% read the file and send the token anywhere.

no exploits or anything needed here - all because the conditions for keeping a secret were violated.

# # what discord could do

there are a fuckton of options, all standard, all industry practice:

- `safeStorage` in electron - since 2019, used by other NORMAL electron apps
- storing secrets directly through the OS keychain
- confirmation to access the secret (biometrics, master password)
- device fingerprint + binding the token to hardware
- a separate key generated on the device at first login
- security alerts when the environment changes abruptly (new ip, new os, new hardware)
- device-based sessions with confirmation of a new device

this can also be bypassed with full access to the machine, but it's incomparably harder to bypass than just reading one shitty file,
right now the attacker only needs the most primitive access - permission to read files in the home directory.


# # if discord can't

one option - log into discord as a dedicated user, so only that user has permission to read the session database:
with no internal exploits in the discord app - the chance of getting anything stolen is minimal.

on linux this is easy out of the box - the permissions hierarchy has been decent since the system was born.

on windows it's generally possible too, but security isn't as good: permissions only showed up as a crutch with the release of Vista, and lots of software still works around them.

but anything's better than leaving a file open to everyone, which discord itself couldn't protect for some unknown reason.

![](/pictures/it/discord_rights.webp)

in `~/.config/discord/Local Storage/leveldb/`

, slap in `grep -r 'mfa\.[a-zA-Z0-9_-]\{20,\}' .`

, the token will most likely be there...
